Aji Panca Chile Powder, Capsicum chinense, is a mild chile powder that measure 500-1,500 Scoville Heat Units (SHU). They are also referred to as aji panca powder, ground aji panca, or Peruvian chili powder.
Aji Panca chiles have been primarily used throughout Peru and Central America, and has been in use in that area for thousands of years. It has a rich burgundy color, notably fruity, smoky flavor, and not much heat. It is excellent for adding depth and nuance to a dish.
Aji Panca Chile Powder is primarily sought after by:
Peruvian and Latin American restaurants;
barbecue restaurants and smokehouses;
cocktail bars;
packaged meat, sauce, meal kit, chocolate, and snack manufacturers;
butchers;
meat processors;
seasoning companies; and
independent spice shops
To view our full selection of chile powders please visit our chile powder page.

Flavor Profile
Aji Panca Chile Powder has a fruity, berry like flavor with aromatic, smoky taste and a mild heat.

How To Use
Aji Panca Chile Powder is a versatile powder that can be used in a great many recipes. It is frequently found in Peruvian and Ecuadorian cuisine. This chile powder goes well with creamy, rich foods, so stir Aji Panca Chile Powder into the cheese sauce when you make Arepas Benedict. Layer it in for added depth when making Fisherman’s Stew. Aji Panca Chile Powder can add sweetness to Peruvian Beef Stew or an extra bit of fruity contrast to Mashed Plantains.
Aji Panca Chiles go beautifully with garlic, onion, oregano, cumin, lime juice, coconut, shallots, turmeric, and coriander. This versatile chile powder is a great accent for chicken, beef, pork, seafood, rice, potatoes, cheese and vegetarian dishes.

Al Pastor Taco Seasoning is an MSG-free blend that is also called al pastor seasoning, pastor seasoning, al pastor seasoning blend, al pastor dry rub, or al pastor rub. This pungent seasoning blend is a combination of mild to medium heat chiles stirred together with aromatic herbs and spices for rich, deep flavor.
Although Al Pastor Seasoning is a Mexican blend, its background comes from Lebanon. The late 19th and early 20th century saw a large influx of Lebanese immigrants to Mexico, who brought their traditional, vertical spit-style cooking with them. Adapting to the food around them they created al pastor. Cooks coat pork in al pastor spices and let it marinate. The pork is placed on a vertical spit and slow-roasted until it is tender and delicious, and shaved off in thin slices, topped with onion, cilantro, and bits of pineapple.
This popular blend is often sought after by:
seasoning companies;
independent spice shops;
Mexican, Latin American, health food, and vegan restaurants;
bars and grills;
sauce, popcorn, and meal mix manufacturers; and
breweries

Flavor Profile
Al Pastor Seasoning is earthy and aromatic with warm, spicy undertones.

How To Use
You don’t need a vertical spit to successfully cook with our Al Pastor Taco Seasoning. Mix in with your favorite taco meat—including Jackfruit, if you’re meatless—and brown in some oil, with diced peppers and onions. Make succulent Pork Burritos. Toss over strips of steak and grill for top-notch Beef Fajitas. Use as a dry rub for your favorite meat; we recommend using 1 tablespoon of Al Pastor seasoning for every pound of meat. Reconstruct classic Al Pastor ingredients into a fun and easy to eat Al Pastor Kebab Salad Bowl. Make a one-pan meal by seasoning Mexican Cornbread with Al Pastor Taco Seasoning. Toss over sliced zucchini or summer squash and roast, then add the roasted vegetables to a Mexican-style pasta with poblanos, corn, and cilantro.

Apple Sausage Blend is a sweet-and-savory sausage seasoning. It is also called apple breakfast sausage blend, chicken apple sausage, apple pork sausage mix, sweet apple sausage seasoning, or country apple sausage blend.
The Apple Sausage Blend is a balanced blend of fruity apple and savory sausage flavors. It works well with a variety of proteins, including pork, turkey, and chicken.
Apple Sausage Blend is sought after by:
sausage, breakfast meat, meal kit, and seasoned snack manufacturers;
breakfast and brunch, farm to table, gastropub, and country style restaurants;
food trucks;
inns and B&Bs;
cafes and diners;
seasoning companies;
butcher shops;
catering companies; and
independent spice shops

Flavor Profile
Apple Sausage Blend is fruity, savory, and herbaceous, with the sweet-tart taste of apples and just a touch of heat.

How To Use
Apple Sausage Blend is a great choice when making a comfort-rich Turkey Sausage Stuffing, either for the holidays or for harvest fairs and events. Sprinkle over bacon cooked with onion and brown sugar to enjoy an Apple Seasoned Bacon Jam. Swap in when making sausage patties with your Sweet Potato Waffles. Give a fruity twist and use this blend when making Spicy Pork Stuffed Peppers. Or give a sweet boost to dinner by choosing Apple Sausage Blend when making Grilled Turkey Burgers.
Ground pork and fatty meats will render out much of the spicy heat in this blend, so if you’re looking to achieve a spicy flavor profile, you may need to add a bit more chile pepper or cayenne.
Apple Sausage Blend is terrific when making sausage, patties, dressings, chilis, smoked foods, marinades, or is used as a rub. Goes well with chicken, turkey, pork, fish, bacon, tofu, breads, potatoes, rice, chestnuts, winter squash, and root vegetables like potatoes, sweet potatoes, and parsnips.

Black Onyx Cocoa Powder is also called black onyx cocoa, onyx cocoa powder, black velvet cocoa powder, or black cocoa powder.
Black Onyx Cocoa Powder is created by being washed in an alkaline solution. Normally, this process, called “Dutching”, is ended when the acids in cocoa are neutralized and the pH of the product is 7. Black Onyx Cocoa Powder is left to wash in alkaline even longer, to a pH of 8. Super-alkalized cocoa powder has a lower fat content than other cocoas. It is milder, with mellower edges, has no acidity so it won’t rise on its own, and is dark brown, verging on black.
Black Onyx Cocoa Powder is popular with:
independent spice shops;
seasoning companies;
manufacturers of bagels and sauces;
food trucks;
coffee shops;
American restaurants;
breweries; and
bakeries

Flavor Profile
Black Onyx Cocoa Powder has a soft, rounded chocolate flavor and a vaguely earthy smell that’s spiked with the fragrance of bitter chocolate.

How To Use
Black Onyx Cocoa Powder is an alkaline so if you’re using this powder for an item that needs leavening, like cake, it needs an acidic base to create rise. You can mix this in with natural cocoa powder, which is acidic, and then use baking soda for rise. Or you can use all Black Onyx Cocoa Powder and mix it with baking powder, which contains both a base and an alkaline and creates its own rise. If you’re making something that requires no leavening, like cookies, frostings, puddings, or in savory applications, then you can use Black Onyx Cocoa Powder on a 1:1 ratio with your usual cocoa powder.
The Dutching process removes residual fats from Black Onyx Cocoa Powder, so it can create a dry product if you don’t counter that by adding something with fat; try butter, shortening, sour cream, or yogurt. It’s also great to use as a coloring agent, so if you need black chocolate icing, replace some of the food dye with Black Onyx Cocoa Powder and let it develop its color for a few hours.
Mix Black Onyx Cocoa Powder with sugar and milk for a deep, dark hot chocolate. Add rich contrast by dusting over a bowl of fruit, or roll it in frozen bananas. Mix into peanut butter smoothies, in a black velvet cake, or to Black Bean Brownies.
Black Onyx Cocoa Powder also can be added to savory dishes. Mix with cinnamon and cumin for a rub for ribs and pork, or stir it into chili. Use it to add deep chocolate flavor to mole, or char some mild peppers and top with a pesto made from almonds, parsley, olive oil, and chocolate. Toss pumpkin seeds with Black Onyx Cocoa Powder, salt, and pepper, and roast. Mix with espresso powder for a super-rich steak rub.

Bloody Mary Mix is a savory, spicy seasoning blend that’s great both for cocktails and for seasoning food. It is also called bloody Mary seasoning, bloody Mary spice mix, or bloody Mary spices.
Use Bloody Mary Mix wherever you want to add a bit of heat and umami dried tomato flavor. The rich flavor of a classic bloody Mary has become a brunch favorite, but it is often dressed with foods like bacon, olives, and shrimp, and so it has developed a reputation as almost a meal in and of itself.
Bloody Mary Mix is popular with:
cocktail and mocktail mix, sauce, marinade, condiment, dressing, and snack manufacturers;
American and seafood restaurants;
bar & grill and café-style restaurants;
seasoning companies;
independent spice shops;
and catering companies

Flavor Profile
Bloody Mary Mix has a deep, umami-sweet tomato base, is pungent and spicy with horseradish and white pepper, with top notes of lemon and basil.

How To Use
Put Bloody Mary Mix on the rim of your bloody Mary, or stir 1/2 to 1 teaspoon of mix--depending on how spicy you want it--into 4 ounces of tomato juice and 2 ounces of vodka to create a classic bloody Mary cocktail; garnish with a squeeze or two of lemon juice and a few dashes of Worcestershire sauce. Infuse into vodka and muddle with basil to make a Bloody Mary Caprese Cocktail. Or stir it into a hearty Bouillabaisse for a steamy, spicy fish stew. Toss over shrimp and enjoy Spicy Grilled Shrimp, either in your bloody Mary glass or as an appetizer. Blend with pork to make your own Homemade Sausage Links or swap this in to punch up the dressing in an unforgettable Chicken Muffuletta.
This blend goes well in cocktails, mocktails, compound butter, dressings, marinades, soups, stews, and tossed with shrimp, chicken, or pork.

Breakfast Sausage Seasoning is a sweet and herbaceous, MSG-free sausage seasoning. It is also known as homemade breakfast sausage seasoning, breakfast sausage seasoning, and sausage seasoning mix.
This is a versatile seasoning blend that goes well in cased or loose sausages, or mixed into various ground meats and side dishes.
Breakfast Sausage Seasoning is popular with:
independent spice shops;
seasoning companies;
olive oil shops;
butchers;
breweries;
caterers; and
sauce manufacturers

Flavor Profile
Breakfast Sausage Seasoning is herbaceous, with a subtle heat and licorice flavor.

How To Use
Breakfast Sausage Seasoning can be used instead of the Campfire Sausage Seasoning in aSausage Breakfast Bowl and in our Dirty Vegetarian Farro for a main course reminiscent of Louisiana’s famous dirty rice. Breakfast Sausage Seasoning goes great with eggs and Tex-Mex Hashbrowns and can help deliver a classic Midwest comfort food breakfast. Another way to use Breakfast Sausage Seasoning is to add it to Sweet Apple Chicken Sausage and Roasted Vegetables in place of the herbs called for in the recipe.
When making sausage, we recommend 1 1/2-2 Tablespoons of Breakfast Sausage Seasoning per pound of meat, since sausage tends to be more intensely seasoned that other dishes.
Breakfast sausage seasoning is a versatile seasoning that can be used in both meat dishes and in vegetable dishes. Add to pasta sauces and soups, eggs, rice, dressings, marinades, roasted vegetables, and roasted or ground meats.

Buffalo Maple Seasoning is an MSG-free seasoning blend that’s sweet and spicy, and loaded with flavor. It is also called sweet Buffalo seasoning or maple Buffalo seasoning.
This blend is a product of 100% American culinary innovation; maple syrup is an American product and buffalo sauce was created in Buffalo, NY. Buffalo Maple Seasoning expands on the ever-growing popularity of spicy food in general and Buffalo flavoring in particular. Maple pairs surprisingly well with classic Buffalo seasoning, giving it a well-rounded, sweet earthiness.
Buffalo Maple Seasoning is a natural fit for:
snack, condiment, sauce, jerky, and frozen meal manufacturers;
chicken, bar & grill, and barbecue restaurants;
brewpubs and breweries;
catering companies;
butchers;
seasoning companies; and
independent spice shops
We also have classic Buffalo Wing Rub available.

Flavor Profile
Buffalo Maple Seasoning is spicy, sweet, and pungent, with an earthy undertone.

How To Use
Use Buffalo Maple Seasoning wherever you’d use classic Buffalo-inspired flavor. Enjoy a batch of Buffalo Maple Wings for game day or any day. Serve that with Buffalo Maple Sauce. Bake into a shareable Buffalo Maple Chicken Chili Corn Dip. Or swap it in when making Buffalo Chicken Sandwiches or Buffalo Chicken Quesadillas for a new twist on some old favorites. This would also be excellent in a spicy dressing for winter salads or squash, tossed into chili, blended into compound butter for breads and cornbreads, or sprinkled over bacon and baked for an unexpected breakfast treat.
Buffalo Maple Seasoning is great on chicken, wings, pork, tofu, and smoked meats. Add to roasted or grilled vegetables. Terrific with dips, sauces, dressings, and marinades.

Buffalo Wing Rub is an MSG-free, sweet-heat centered seasoning blend. It is also known as dry buffalo wing seasoning, buffalo dry rub, buffalo wing seasoning mix, or buffalo rub.
This blend creates a quick and easy buffalo wing recipe, which is great if you want to bake or fry your wings, or even make them from tofu or cauliflower.
Buffalo Wing Rub is popular with:
independent spice shops;
seasoning companies;
dip mix, snack, and soda and water manufacturers;
barbecue and fine dining restaurants;
brewpubs;
catering companies;
butchers; and
olive oil shops

Flavor Profile
Buffalo Wing Rub balances sweetness, earthy warmth, and smoky heat.

How to Use
Use Buffalo Wing Rub on your next batch of chicken wings, or try it on vegetarian Cauliflower Buffalo Wings. Deconstruct wing night into a tasty Buffalo Chicken Quesadilla or make game day Buffalo Chicken Sandwiches. Sprinkle some on a batch of French fries, roasted cauliflower, or mix with mayo or sour cream for a quick dip.
Use on chicken, tofu, cauliflower, roasted, grilled, or fried meats and vegetables. Makes a great, spicy-sweet rub for smoked meats and vegetables. Stir into dips, sauces, marinades.

Our Cajun Seasoning is the perfect blend of zesty, spicy, savory flavors that are sure to kick any dish up a delicious notch. This salt- and MSG-free seasoning is sometimes referred to as bulk cajun seasoning, cajun seasoning bulk, Louisiana cajun seasoning or salt-free cajun seasoning.
Cajun and Creole are not the same. Creole cuisine evolved from classic French or Spanish cooking techniques, fused with local foods. We also have Creole Seasoning available.As a rule of thumb, Cajun dishes are one-pot creations like gumbos, dirty rice, and jambalaya, made with local favorites like fried catfish and a spicy smoked sausage called andouille. Cajun cooking features simple ingredients and pork fat is a base in many dishes.
Frequent buyers of Cajun Seasoning are:
seasoning companies;
olive oil shops;
independent spice shops;
American and Mexican restaurants;
food trucks;
bar & grill restaurants;
diners;
brewers; and
those who manufacturers sauces and gourmet condiments
We also have Spicy Cajun Seasoning and Cajun Maple Seasoning available.

Flavor Profile
Bold and spicy pepper with savory garlic and onion undertones.

How To Use
You can make your own dirty rice or experiment with Dirty Vegetarian Farro. Enjoy Cajun Seasoning in Chicken and Sausage Gumbo or jambalaya, or bake up an appetizer tray of Crispy Cajun Chicken Wings. Make it a meal with Crispy Cajun Chicken or serve up a side of Cajun Grilled Sweet Potatoes. Use our Cajun Seasoning anywhere else you want some down-on-the-bayou flavor – on the grill, on your broiled shrimp or stirred into mayo to spice up a turkey sandwich.

California Minced Garlic, Allium sativum, is garlic that is picked, cleaned, minced, and dried. It is also called minced garlic, dried minced garlic, or minced dried garlic.
California Minced Garlic is an easy way to add bold garlic flavor and terrific texture to foods. It is a larger granule, as the pieces measure between 8-20 mesh.
California Minced Garlic is often sought after by:
sauce, frozen meal, and snack food manufacturers;
BBQ and bar & grill restaurants;
cafes;
bakeries;
seasoning companies;
independent spice shops;
butchers; and
catering companies
Please visit our garlic page to view our complete lineup of garlic products.
One fresh garlic clove = ¼ teaspoon of granulated garlic, 1/8 teaspoon of garlic powder, or ½ teaspoon of minced garlic.

Flavor Profile
California Minced Garlic delivers a typically robust, umami garlic flavor.

How To Use
California Minced Garlic is the garlic to choose when you want to add texture as well as flavor to recipes or customized seasoning blends. Add minced garlic directly to cooking liquids like sauces, soups, or chilis. It is great for pickling, marinades, dressings, and chunky gravies. It can also be rehydrated and sauteed with an enormous array of meats, vegetables, starches, and casseroles.
California Minced Garlic goes well with foods from around the world and pairs particularly well with basil, ginger, chile peppers, turmeric, meat, tofu, beans, olive oil, leafy greens, onions, and spinach.

California Onion Powder, Allium cepa, is also called bulk onion powder, wholesale onion powder, onion powder, or California style onion powder. It comes from sweet onions picked at their peak flavor, then dehydrated and milled into a fine, blendable powder.
Onions come in many forms, and they get their potent, tear-inducing pungency from sulfur-based compounds. A member of the Allium family, they related to chives and shallots, which helps explain why these vegetables are all so pungent. Onions rank fourth in US vegetable consumption, behind heavy hitters like potatoes, tomatoes, and sweet corn. Their versatility gives them enormous appeal, appearing in everything from bread toppings to meatballs.
Our California Onion Powder is regularly requested by:
butchers;
breweries;
caterers;
sauce and meal mix manufacturers;
cafe, BBQ, salad, and American restaurants;
seasoning companies;
olive oil shops; and
independent spice shops
1 Tablespoon of Onion Powder = 1 Tablespoon Granulated Onion, 1-2/3 Tablespoons of Minced Onion, or one medium fresh onion, chopped.
Please visit our onion page to view our full selection of onion products.

Flavor Profile
Onion Powder is sweet and piquant. It delivers a savory, deep onion flavor without the aggressive sharpness of raw onion. The dehydrating and milling process enhances the onion’s natural sugars.

How to Use
Onion Powder is best used where it can be blended smoothly into a dish during the meal preparations. Add it to Glazed Meatballs with Soba Noodles for a fragrant cold salad. Add it to your mixture for rich and flavorful Turkey Burgers. Whisk it into a DIY Dry Rib Rub. Stir a tablespoon into Pineapple Turmeric Sauerkraut and let it season your pickling. It’s wonderful with savory bread, so incorporate it into Naan or include it in the buttery topping for Dill Focaccia. Because onions are found just about everywhere, feel free to add this into marinades or sauces, particularly if you want a fresh bite of onion as the accent at the end.
Onions are phenomenally cooperative with other herbs and spices. While they go with just about any savory food or cuisine imaginable, they pair exceptionally well with garlic, ginger, chiles, vinegar, tomatoes, tarragon, red wine, turmeric, and yogurt.

Campfire Sausage Seasoning is an MSG-free, smoky, sweet-heat sausage seasoning. It is also referred to as smoky sausage seasoning and smoky pork sausage seasoning.
This seasoning blend was inspired by the rich flavors and aromas experienced around a campfire. It’s got smoky heat, a bit of sugar, and even a grassy pop from celery seed, all enhanced by smoked salt. Use this wherever you use sausage seasoning for a spicy-sweet burst of intense flavor.
Campfire Sausage Seasoning is a popular seasoning for:
seasoning companies;
independent spice shops;
jam and gourmet jam manufacturers;
American and bar & grill restaurants;
food trucks;
breweries; and
olive oil shops

Flavor Profile
Campfire Sausage Seasoning is a bold seasoning blend. It’s loaded with smoky, sweet heat, earthy warmth, and crunchy, flavorful aromatics.

How To Use
Campfire Sausage Seasoning was made with pork in mind. Mix it into ground pork and make patties for a Campfire Sausage and Egg Breakfast Bowl. Stuff casings for Campfire Sausage Links, and then nestle it into a skillet and enjoy Campfire Skillet Mac & Cheese. Swap this is to make a Sausage and Polenta Casserole. It’s also terrific as a chili seasoning, so stir it into some Spicy Slow Cooker Chili or Easy Homemade Chili for some hard-to-beat sweet-heat smokiness.
When making sausage, we recommend 1 1/2-2 Tablespoons of Campfire Sausage Seasoning per pound of meat, since sausage tends to be more intensely seasoned that other dishes.
Campfire Sausage Seasoning is good on pork, beef, turkey, and chicken. Stir into hamburgers, meatballs, or meatloaf. Rub on wings, ribs, and chops for roasting or grilling. Give a spicy twist to queso, alfredo, or cream cheese. Add to eggs, rice dishes, and hearty grilled or roasted vegetables like broccoli, broccoli rabe, or peppers and onions.

Cardamom Seeds, Elettaria cardamomum, are the small brown and black seeds found inside the green cardamom pods. They are also called cardamom seed, decorticated cardamom, cardamon seed, cardamum, or cardamom pod seeds.
Papery green cardamom pods are technically a fruit, and the papery pod itself, while rich in flavor, is inedible. Each pod contains approximately 12 seeds, also rich in flavor, that can be toasted and ground or crushed for use in recipes.
Cardamom Seeds are often purchased by:
bakeries;
coffee and tea manufacturers;
coffee shops;
fine dining restaurants;
butcher shops;
distilleries;
olive oil shops; and
breweries
We also carry Ground Cardamom and Cardamom Pods, as well as Organic Cardamom Seeds and Organic Ground Cardamom.

Flavor Profile
Cardamom Seeds are complex, peppery, and floral, with elements of eucalyptus, mint, and citrus.

How To Use
Cardamom Seeds should be cracked or crushed in a mortar and pestle before use, and to bring out full flavor, toast in a dry pan before using. Cardamom Seeds are a staple in Indian cuisine but can be found around the world. Add them to turkey dumplings, pilafs, meatballs, or pork roast. Cardamom Seeds also shine in desserts, so toast and grind them to add to ice cream or Swedish Christmas bread.
Use Cardamom Seeds when making curries, DIY spice blends, rice dishes, mulled wine and cider, brewing, with chicken, lamb, fish, or pork, and when grilling, roasting, and baking.
Cardamom Seeds work well in combination with caraway, chili powder, cinnamon, coriander, cumin, ginger, paprika, cilantro, garlic, orange, lemon, maple, and pepper.

Carolina Gold BBQ Rub is an MSG-free, mustard-based barbecue rub and base for sauces and dressings. It is also known as Carolina mustard barbeque rub, Carolina mustard BBQ, mustard BBQ rub, or Carolina gold barbeque rub.
The central region of South Carolina is famous for its mustard-based barbecue, which may have evolved thanks to the arrival of German and French immigrants in the 1700s. This area is affectionately referred to as the “mustard belt”.
Carolina Gold BBQ Rub is popular with:
barbecue and bar & grill-style restaurants;
food trucks;
sauce, condiment, snack, meal subscription, and jerky manufacturers;
seasoning companies;
butchers;
independent spice shops; and
catering companies

Flavor Profile
Carolina Gold BBQ Rub is bold and tangy, with sweet and sour balance and tingly, horseradish-like heat.

How To Use
Nothing says Carolina barbecue like chopped pork; use this rub as a dry marinade and slow-cook some Carolina Gold Smoked Pork Shoulder and then mix up some Carolina Gold BBQ Mustard. Since this blend is vegan you can also use it to make Carolina Gold BBQ Tofu Skewers, and serve it with Carolina Gold-kissed Sweet Potato Dipping Sauce. Substitute Carolina Gold BBQ Rub as the primary seasoning when you make Sweet Apple Chicken Sausage and Roasted Vegetables for a spicy, earthy twist. It is terrific when it’s swapped in to make Crispy Chicken Wings or to season a non-dairy Mustard Potato Salad.
Carolina Gold BBQ Rub pairs well with pork, chicken, firm fish like catfish, perch, or grouper, sausage, potatoes, jackfruit, tofu, seitan, tempeh, apples, butternut and other winter squash, breads, rice, spinach, and lentils.

Celery Seed, Apium graveolens, also known as celery seeds or bulk celery seeds come from wild celery, a relative of the kind of celery available in grocery stores.
Tiny celery seeds are actually the fruit of the celery plant. They are so small, it takes roughly 45,000 seeds to make up one ounce of product.
Celery seed is popular with:
American, seafood, and BBQ restaurants;
brewpubs;
seasoning companies;
independent spice shops;
butchers;
breweries;
caterers;
sauce and condiment manufacturers; and
fermenters and picklers
One teaspoon of Celery Seed equals 2 tablespoons minced celery tops.
We also offer Ground Celery Seed, Organic Celery Seeds and Organic Ground Celery Seeds.

Flavor Profile
Celery Seed has a hay-like, grassy, pungent, slightly bitter taste and aroma.

How to Use
Celery Seed is pungent and can overpower a dish. Toast celery seed in a dry pan to wake up the volatile oils and add it to potato salad, pimento cheese, succotash or added to Pickled Red Onions. Grind and add to a DIY Dry Rib Rub. Mix with pepper and lemon peel to season strong-tasting fish, like salmon. Add zing to comfort favorites like Split Pea Soup. This is a must-have for homemade sausages, bread & butter pickles, Homemade Pickles, or Spicy Cabbage Slaw.
Use the distinctive, pungent flavor of celery seeds in your bread, brines, casseroles, chicken soup, coleslaw, eggs, meat, pickles, vegetable dishes, gravies, salad dressings, sauces, sauerkraut, stuffing, stews and fresh tomato juice.
Celery Seed goes well with onion, nutmeg, parsley, bell peppers, carrots, turmeric, chicken, sage, cumin, soy sauce, ginger, and vinegar.

Chai Baking Spice Blend is a fragrant, salt-free and MSG-free seasoning blend. It is also known as chai spice powder, chai masala blend, masala chai, or chai spice mix.
Chai is a highly spiced, fragrant, aromatic spiced tea that originated in India. It is a combination of black tea commonly found growing in Assam, mixed with the spices that also grow in India. Chai appeared on US coffee shop menus in the early 1990s, usually as a liquid spice mix. This blend delivers all the flavors of a coffee shop mix, in versatile powder form.
Chai Baking Spice is popular with:
breweries;
coffee and tea shops;
cafes;
seasoning companies;
manufacturers of baking mixes;
independent spice shops; and
olive oil shops

Flavor Profile
Chai Baking Spice tastes nutty and woodsy with a sweet warmth.

How to Use
Chai Baking Spice Blend is wide open for multiple uses. It can, of course, be used to create a Masala Chai. It can also be used as the primary seasoning blend in spice cookies swap in as the main flavoring when making Healthy Carrot Cake Squares. Canners can use this blend to make Chai Spiced Peaches, or swap out peaches and use plums or apricots. Make breakfast a breeze by using it in Chai Spiced Overnight Oats. Mix with bananas for Chai Banana Muffins or put bananas and Chai Baking Spice into a blender with yogurt for a smoothie. Make a simple syrup with Chai Baking Spice Blend and blend it with bourbon or whiskey to enjoy a Chai Baking Spice Old Fashioned.
This blend has savory uses, too. Sprinkle on cubed butternut squash and roast, and top risotto or a creamy pasta with roasted cubes. Stir into rice, or toss over pork chops and pan-fry. Craft cocktailers can steep this in syrup for a chai-spiced simple syrup that will make unforgettable old-fashioneds.

Cheesy Jalapeno Seasoning is a spicy, umami-rich seasoning blend inspired by the local Philadelphia peppers known as long hots. It is also called cheesy long hot seasoning, spicy cheese blend, jalapeno seasoning blend, or jalapeno popper spices.
Long hots are long Italian peppers, usually braised and then chopped and put on sandwiches. They can be quite spicy depending on the type and pungency of pepper used, though they’re usually playfully hot. They’re occasionally stuffed with pork or cheese and served as a side dish on their own.
Businesses interested in Cheesy Jalapeno Seasoning include:
snack, chip, bagel, sauce, and meal kit manufacturers;
seasoning companies;
American, BBQ, and breakfast or bagel restaurants;
bar and grill style restaurants;
food trucks;
independent spice shops; and
catering companies

Flavor Profile
Cheesy Jalapeno Seasoning is earthy, herbaceous, salty, with a mid-range heat that lingers.

How To Use
Cheesy Jalapeno Seasoning is a great snack topping. We used it to make Cheesy Jalapeno Popcorn, but it would also be terrific tossed over potato chips or dusted across French fries or Roasted Parsnip Fries. It goes beautifully with pork and, also, with peppers, so enjoy it on Spicy Pork Stuffed Peppers. Strong flavors, like salmon, go well with this blend, so mix it into Grilled Salmon Burgers with Horseradish Dressing. Or, swap in as the main seasoning when making Crispy Baked Chicken.
Cheesy Jalapeno Seasoning is a terrific addition to snack foods, savory desserts, breads, breading, pork, chicken, tofu, seafood, pasta, peppers, potatoes, parsnips, sweet potatoes, carrots, and broccoli. Perfect for seasoned coatings, marinades, sauces, dressings, and roasted and grilled vegetables and meats.

Chili Lime Seasoning is an MSG-free seasoning blend that combines sweet heat, savory aromatics, and tart citrus. It is also known as chili lime powder, chili lime seasoning, or chili lime rub.
This versatile seasoning blend is a flavor-packed powerhouse. It’s a top option when making barbecue rubs and grilling choices, it’s a terrific blend when you want to create an unforgettable cocktail or mocktail, and it gives an enchanting, spicy boost to sweet fruits.
Chili Lime Seasoning is popular with:
independent spice shops;
olive oil shops;
seasoning companies;
bar and grill-style restaurants;
food trucks;
Latin American and Mediterranean restaurants; and
bitters, syrups, kettle corn, popcorn, and cannabis edible manufacturers

Flavor Profile
Chili Lime Seasoning tastes tart, zesty, and earthy with a playful, fruity heat.

How to Use
Sprinkle over watermelon, pineapple, cucumber, mango, jicama, or papaya. It is the key to making a fruity, pungent Chamoy Sauce. Chamoy is a terrific base for barbecue sauce, goes wonderfully on vanilla ice cream, and is the star ingredient in a sweet, spicy, and refreshing Mangonada. Give Tropical Mango Salsa or Sweet Cantaloupe Salsa a spicy, citrus boost. Add it to savory foods and put it in this hearty Salsa Soup, or add it to some meat and enjoy a cheesy Chili Lime Steak Quesadilla. You can also use it to give a surprising kick to the New York-style hot dog onions that top our Hot Dogs with Porter Mustard.
This blend adds bold flavor to beef, chicken, pork, seafood, vegetables, ceviche, empanadas, or tamales. It gives unexpected zest to dressings, guacamole, marinades, sauces, chips, and popcorn.

Chili Powder Mild is a salt-free, sugar-free, MSG-free seasoning blend that brings warming, earthy chile flavors to a dish with minimal heat. It is also referred to as mild chili powder, chili powder, or mild chili powder seasoning.
The first commercial blend of chili powder in the U.S. was created by William Gebhardt in 1894. Gebhardt had a saloon in New Braunfels, Texas and his chili was the town's favorite dish. To avoid seasonal supply issues, Gebhardt imported chiles from Mexico and processed them through a small meat grinder, so he could always have access to the flavor and proportions he needed.
